#pragma once

// std::tuple for the supported subset (M10).
//
// The shape is a recursive variadic class template: `tuple<int, char>` is
// `{ int head; tuple<char> tail; }`, and `tuple<>` is the empty primary.
//
// Construction is in place rather than through a factory, because a factory
// returns the tuple by value and KR16 rejects aggregate returns.
//
// Element access is named rather than indexed: the free functions `tuple_head` and
// `tuple_tail`, and the members `.head` / `.tail`. `get<I>` is not provided because
// it needs a member template or a partial specialisation over a non-type argument,
// and this front end has neither; both are measured in implementation.md. Reach
// element k by chaining members (`pair.tail.head`).
//
// Four limits are real, and each is measured rather than assumed:
//
//   * **There is no element constructor.** `tuple(T first, Rest... rest)` is a
//     *class member* whose signature carries a pack parameter, and this front end
//     expands such a signature for one element only: `tuple<int, char> p(41, 'x')`
//     compiles and then fails to link with `undefined symbol 'tuple_ctor_0_value'`,
//     because the call binds to the pattern's constructor rather than an instance's.
//     Construct by default and assign instead, which works at any arity:
//
//         tuple<int, char, long> t;
//         t.head = 1; t.tail.head = 'y'; t.tail.tail.head = 9;
//
//   * **No by-value factory.** `make_tuple` would return the tuple by value, which
//     KR16 rejects as an aggregate return, and it would be built on the constructor
//     above. It is provided on neither target rather than on one.
//
//   * **An empty tuple cannot be a local.** `tuple<> t;` emits a call to the
//     pattern's constructor and fails to link with the same undefined symbol. An
//     empty tuple reached as a member is fine -- `tuple_size(pair.tail.tail)` is
//     zero -- so the empty type exists and only a local of it is affected.
//
//   * **Do not chain the free functions on one line.**
//     `tuple_head(tuple_tail(pair))` crashes the compiler: a reference-returning
//     call passed as an argument is an unrelated defect. The member form
//     (`tuple_tail(pair).head`) works.

namespace std {

template <typename... Ts>
struct tuple {
};

template <typename T, typename... Rest>
struct tuple<T, Rest...> {
    T head;
    tuple<Rest...> tail;

    tuple() : head(), tail() {}
};

// The number of elements, for the primary and for every specialization: the pack
// is the whole parameter list, so one function answers both.
template <typename... Ts>
int tuple_size(tuple<Ts...>& value) {
    return sizeof...(Ts);
}

template <typename T, typename... Rest>
T& tuple_head(tuple<T, Rest...>& value) {
    return value.head;
}

template <typename T, typename... Rest>
tuple<Rest...>& tuple_tail(tuple<T, Rest...>& value) {
    return value.tail;
}

}  // namespace std
